      "status": "affected",
      "action_statement": "Update uv to 0.12.17-0 or later.",
      "status_notes": "CVE-2026-123613 (GHSA-2mjx-qc3c-rqvc, RUSTSEC-2026-0285): rustls 0.23.13 to 0.23.44 accepts TLS 1.3 handshake messages sent at the wrong encryption level when they follow a key-changing message in the same record, so a peer can send messages that must be encrypted, such as an EncryptedExtensions packed into the ServerHello record, in plaintext without the connection being rejected; the handshake transcript is still authenticated. Fixed in rustls 0.23.45: https://github.com/rustls/rustls/security/advisories/GHSA-2mjx-qc3c-rqvc, https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0285.html. Read the crate list that cargo-auditable embeds in /usr/bin/uv and /usr/bin/uvx of all 84 published uv packages in the DHI Debian repository, 0.11.22-0 to 0.13.9-2 for amd64 and arm64, each verified against the checksum in the package index; no debug-symbol packages are published for this source. The crate list of /usr/bin/uv names rustls 0.23.40 to 0.23.43 in builds 0.11.22-0 to 0.12.15-0, rustls 0.23.45 in 0.12.17-0 to 0.13.4-0, and rustls 0.23.46 in every build from 0.13.4-1 on; each binary's list names a single rustls version. Only /usr/bin/uv contains rustls code: /usr/bin/uvx is a small launcher that runs uv, and although its embedded list names the same crates, a strings search finds no rustls source paths in it in the 0.11.22-0, 0.12.15-0, 0.12.17-0, 0.13.4-0, 0.13.4-1 and 0.13.9-2 packages for either architecture, while the same search finds them in /usr/bin/uv and finds other crates' paths in /usr/bin/uvx. The builds up to 0.12.15-0 are affected and every build from 0.12.17-0 on is fixed."
    },
